\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement problem}

A recurring pattern in applied combinatorics is the following. One has a finite
population $\Omega$ --- genotypes, database records, group elements, integers in
a range --- and a family of coarse, cheap \emph{readings} of that population,
each taking values in a small finite alphabet. Individually the readings are
almost useless; jointly they may or may not identify individuals. The practical
question is how much identifying power the family has, and how that power grows
as readings are added.

The natural currency is information. If we sample an individual $x$ uniformly
from $\Omega$ and observe the tuple of readings, the mutual information between
$x$ and the observation measures, in bits, the average reduction in the log-size
of the candidate set. Since the readings are deterministic, the conditional
entropy of the observation given $x$ vanishes, and the mutual information equals
the entropy of the observation. This is a purely combinatorial quantity: the
entropy of the block-size distribution of the partition of $\Omega$ into level
sets.

We call a bounded-alphabet reading a \emph{dial}, a family of dials a
\emph{battery}, and the resulting quantity the \emph{joint capacity} of the
battery.

\subsection{The measurement to be audited}

A four-dial battery with moduli $31$, $23$, $9$, $8$ was read on a population of
several thousand individuals. Three nested sub-batteries were reported:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
sub-battery & cells $M = \prod_{i \in S} m_i$ & measured $C(S)$ \\
\midrule
moduli $\{31, 23\}$ & $713$ & $7.9455$ \\
moduli $\{31, 23, 9\}$ & $6\,417$ & $10.4462$ \\
moduli $\{31, 23, 9, 8\}$ & $51\,336$ & $12.1080$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Per-dial capacities ranged over a factor of eighty: a modulus-$11$ dial carried
$3.46$ bits, a modulus-$31$ dial carried $0.04$ bits. A binary
``which-factor'' readout was capped at $0.4677$ bits. The verdict attached to
the experiment was that joint capacity scales with battery size, replicating an
earlier finding on an independent population.

Our thesis is that a replication of this kind must be decomposed before it can
be believed to say anything: some of what was observed is a theorem and could
not have failed, while some is a genuine measurement of the population.

\section{The finitary Shannon calculus}

Throughout, $\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set with $N = |\Omega|$, and a
\emph{statistic} is any function $f : \Omega \to A$ into an arbitrary set.

\begin{definition}[fibres and counts]
For $a \in A$ the \emph{fibre} is $f^{-1}(a)$, of cardinality $n_a = \cnt_f(a)$;
the \emph{image} $\img(f)$ is the finite set of attained values. Then
$\sum_{a \in \img(f)} n_a = N$ and $n_a \ge 1$ for attained $a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[empirical entropy]
The \emph{empirical entropy} of $f$, in nats, is
\[
H(f) \;=\; \sum_{a \in \img(f)} \frac{n_a}{N}\,\log\frac{N}{n_a},
\]
and the \emph{capacity in bits} is $C(f) = H(f)/\log 2$.
\end{definition}

$H(f)$ is the Shannon entropy of the push-forward under $f$ of the uniform
measure on $\Omega$; since $f$ is deterministic, $H(f) = I(x; f(x))$ for $x$
uniform. If $f$ is constant then $H(f) = 0$; if $f$ is injective then
$H(f) = \log N$.

\begin{proposition}[non-negativity]
$H(f) \ge 0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Each attained $a$ has $1 \le n_a \le N$, so $\log(N/n_a) \ge 0$ and all terms are
non-negative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[alphabet ceiling / maximum entropy]\label{thm:maxent}
Let $K = |\img(f)|$. Then $H(f) \le \log K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $p_a = n_a/N$. Apply $\log t \le t-1$ with $t = 1/(p_a K)$:
\[
\log\frac{1}{p_a} = \log\frac{1}{p_a K} + \log K \le \Bigl(\frac{1}{p_a K} - 1\Bigr) + \log K .
\]
Multiply by $p_a \ge 0$ and sum over the $K$ attained values, using
$\sum_a p_a = 1$:
\[
H(f) \le \log K + \Bigl(\sum_a \tfrac{1}{K} - \sum_a p_a\Bigr) = \log K + (1-1) = \log K. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[sample ceiling / sparse-table bias]\label{cor:sample}
$H(f) \le \log N$ for every statistic $f$, regardless of its alphabet.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$K = |\img(f)| \le |\Omega| = N$ and $\log$ is monotone.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:sample} is the formal content of sparse-table bias: an
entropy estimated from an $N$-row table cannot exceed $\log_2 N$ bits no matter
how many cells the table has.

\begin{lemma}[fibrewise splitting]\label{lem:fiber}
For any map $g$ on labels,
\[
H(f) = \sum_{b \in \img(g \circ f)} \ \sum_{\substack{a \in \img(f) \\ g(a) = b}} \frac{n_a}{N}\log\frac{N}{n_a},
\qquad \sum_{a : g(a) = b} n_a = \cnt_{g \circ f}(b).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The map $a \mapsto g(a)$ sends $\img(f)$ into $\img(g \circ f)$, so the defining
sum may be grouped along its fibres; the count identity is the disjoint
decomposition of a coarse fibre into fine fibres.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[data processing]\label{thm:dpi}
$H(g \circ f) \le H(f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix an attained $b$ with coarse count $n_b^{\mathrm{c}}$. Every fine label $a$
over $b$ has $n_a \le n_b^{\mathrm{c}}$, hence
$\log(N/n_b^{\mathrm{c}}) \le \log(N/n_a)$, and so, using
Lemma~\ref{lem:fiber},
\[
\frac{n_b^{\mathrm{c}}}{N}\log\frac{N}{n_b^{\mathrm{c}}}
= \sum_{a : g(a)=b} \frac{n_a}{N}\log\frac{N}{n_b^{\mathrm{c}}}
\le \sum_{a : g(a)=b} \frac{n_a}{N}\log\frac{N}{n_a}.
\]
Summing over $b$ and applying Lemma~\ref{lem:fiber} again gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[strict data processing]\label{thm:sdpi}
If there exist $x, y \in \Omega$ with $f(x) \ne f(y)$ but
$g(f(x)) = g(f(y))$, then $H(g \circ f) < H(f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $b = g(f(x))$. The coarse fibre over $b$ contains the two distinct fine
labels $f(x)$ and $f(y)$, both of positive count, so
$n_{f(x)} < n_b^{\mathrm{c}}$ strictly and hence
$\log(N/n_b^{\mathrm{c}}) < \log(N/n_{f(x)})$. Multiplying by the positive
weight $n_{f(x)}/N$ makes the $a = f(x)$ term in the proof of
Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} strict; a sum of valid inequalities with one strict term
is strict.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[relabelling is free]\label{prop:relabel}
If $g$ is injective on $\img(f)$ then $H(g \circ f) = H(f)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[separation implies positivity]
If $f(x) \ne f(y)$ for some $x, y$, then $H(f) > 0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The fibre over $f(x)$ omits $y$, so $n_{f(x)} < N$ and that term is strictly
positive, while all others are non-negative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[subadditivity]\label{thm:subadd}
For statistics $f, g$ on the same population,
$H\bigl(x \mapsto (f(x), g(x))\bigr) \le H(f) + H(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $p_c$ for the joint block probabilities, $c = (a,b)$, and $q_a, r_b$ for
the marginals; the joint image lies in $\img(f) \times \img(g)$, and summing
$p_{(a,b)}$ over $b$ gives $q_a$ (and symmetrically). Apply $\log t \le t-1$
with $t = q_a r_b / p_c$:
\[
\log\frac{1}{p_c} = \log\frac{1}{q_a} + \log\frac{1}{r_b} + \log\frac{q_a r_b}{p_c}
\le \log\frac{1}{q_a} + \log\frac{1}{r_b} + \frac{q_a r_b}{p_c} - 1 .
\]
Multiply by $p_c$ and sum: the first two groups collapse to $H(f)$ and $H(g)$ by
the marginal identities, and the remainder is
$\sum_c q_a r_b - \sum_c p_c \le 1 - 1 = 0$ because the joint image is contained
in the product of the images.
\end{proof}

The slack in Theorem~\ref{thm:subadd} is exactly the mutual information
$I(f;g)$; equality holds iff $f$ and $g$ are independent under the uniform
measure.

\section{Batteries of dials and monotone scaling}

\begin{definition}[dial]
A \emph{dial} on $\Omega$ is a pair $(m, r)$ with $m \ge 1$ the \emph{modulus}
and $r : \Omega \to \mathbb{N}$ the \emph{reading}, subject to $r(x) < m$ for all
$x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[battery, joint reading, joint capacity]
A \emph{battery} is a family $d = (d_i)_{i \in \iota}$ of dials over a finite
index set. For $S \subseteq \iota$ the \emph{joint reading} is
$\jt_S(x) = (r_i(x))_{i \in S} \in \mathbb{N}^S$ and the \emph{joint capacity} is
$C(S) = H(\jt_S)/\log 2$ bits. The \emph{per-dial capacity} is
$c_i = H(r_i)/\log 2$.
\end{definition}

Always $C(S) \ge 0$, and $C(\emptyset) = 0$ since the empty tuple is constant.

\begin{lemma}[restriction is a coarsening]\label{lem:restr}
For $S \subseteq T$ and the coordinate projection
$\pi_{S,T} : \mathbb{N}^T \to \mathbb{N}^S$ we have
$\jt_S = \pi_{S,T} \circ \jt_T$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[monotone scaling]\label{thm:mono}
If $S \subseteq T$ then $C(S) \le C(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Lemma~\ref{lem:restr} with Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} and divide by
$\log 2 > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[strict scaling criterion]\label{thm:strict}
Let $S \subseteq T$. Suppose $x, y \in \Omega$ satisfy $\jt_S(x) = \jt_S(y)$ ---
every dial of $S$ confuses $x$ and $y$ --- while $r_i(x) \ne r_i(y)$ for some
$i \in T$. Then $C(S) < C(T)$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The dial $i$ separates $x$ and $y$, so $\jt_T(x) \ne \jt_T(y)$; applying
$\pi_{S,T}$ recovers $\jt_S(x) = \jt_S(y)$, so the coarsening merges them, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:sdpi} applies with $f = \jt_T$, $g = \pi_{S,T}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[the criterion is not vacuous]
Take $\Omega = \{0,1,2,3\}$ with the parity dial $r_0(x) = x \bmod 2$ and the
half dial $r_1(x) = \lfloor x/2 \rfloor$, both of modulus $2$. Parity confuses
$0$ and $2$; the half dial separates them. Hence $C(\{0\}) < C(\{0,1\})$;
explicitly $1 < 2$ bits.
\end{example}

\section{The multiplicative (CRT) ceiling}

\begin{lemma}[cell count]\label{lem:cells}
$|\img(\jt_S)| \le \prod_{i \in S} m_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The joint reading takes values in $\prod_{i \in S}\{0,\dots,m_i-1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[multiplicative ceiling]\label{thm:crt}
$C(S) \le \log_2 \prod_{i \in S} m_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:maxent} gives $C(S) \le \log_2 |\img(\jt_S)|$; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:cells}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[sample ceiling]\label{thm:pop}
$C(S) \le \log_2 N$ independently of the moduli.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Corollary~\ref{cor:sample} applied to $\jt_S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[which ceiling binds]
The effective ceiling is $\min\{\log_2 \prod_{i\in S} m_i, \log_2 N\}$. When
$N \ll M = \prod m_i$ the sample ceiling binds and the measurement speaks about
the population; when $M \ll N$ the multiplicative ceiling binds and the
measurement is instrument-limited. Near $N \approx M$ neither bound is tight:
for a uniformly random assignment of $N$ rows to $M$ cells the expected number of
occupied cells is $M(1 - (1-1/M)^N)$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[non-coprime moduli]
Lemma~\ref{lem:cells} uses only alphabet sizes, so Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} holds
for arbitrary moduli; but for genuine residue dials on a cyclic population,
non-coprime moduli make the joint reading factor through
$\mathbb{Z}/\lcm(m_i)$, whence the true ceiling $\log_2 \lcm(m_i)$ is strictly
smaller. For the audited moduli $31, 23, 9, 8$, which are pairwise coprime,
$\lcm = \prod = 51\,336$ and no correction applies.
\end{remark}

\section{Sharpness: the multiplicative ceiling is attained}

\begin{theorem}[separating statistics saturate]\label{thm:inj}
If $f : \Omega \to A$ is injective then $H(f)/\log 2 = \log_2 N$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every attained label has $n_a = 1$, hence $|\img(f)| = N$ and
$H(f) = \sum_a \frac{1}{N}\log N = \log N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chinese Remainder witness]\label{thm:crtwitness}
Let $\Omega = \mathbb{Z}/31 \times \mathbb{Z}/23$, a population of exactly
$N = 713$ individuals, with dials reading the canonical representatives of the
two coordinates, of moduli $31$ and $23$. Then
\[
C(\{1,2\}) = \log_2 713 = \log_2(31 \cdot 23),
\]
so the ceiling of Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} is attained with equality.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The joint reading is injective, since the canonical representative map
$\mathbb{Z}/m \to \{0,\dots,m-1\}$ is a bijection; equivalently, by the Chinese
Remainder Theorem the pair of residues modulo the coprime moduli $31, 23$
determines an element of $\mathbb{Z}/713$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[shortfalls are data]\label{cor:shortfall}
Since Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} is attained, any observed gap between a measured
$C(S)$ and $\log_2 \prod_{i \in S} m_i$ is a property of the population --- a
quantification of collisions in the joint reading --- and not slack in the
inequality.
\end{corollary}

\section{The per-dial budget}

\begin{theorem}[per-dial budget]\label{thm:budget}
$C(S) \le \sum_{i \in S} c_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $S$. For $S = \emptyset$ both sides vanish. For
$S' = \{a\} \cup S$ with $a \notin S$, the reorganisation
$u \mapsto (u_a, (u_i)_{i \in S})$ from $\mathbb{N}^{S'}$ to
$\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}^{S}$ is injective, and composing it with $\jt_{S'}$
gives the pair statistic $x \mapsto (r_a(x), \jt_S(x))$. By
Proposition~\ref{prop:relabel} the entropy is unchanged, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:subadd} yields $C(S') \le c_a + C(S)$, and the inductive
hypothesis finishes the step.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[the slack is mutual information]\label{rem:lossy}
The slack in Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} is a sum of mutual informations among the
dials, so the budget is tight exactly for mutually independent dials.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[increment bound]\label{cor:increment}
For $a \notin S$,
\[
C(\{a\} \cup S) - C(S) \;=\; C\bigl(r_a \mid \jt_S\bigr) \;\le\; c_a ,
\]
where the conditional capacity averages, over blocks of $\jt_S$, the capacity of
$r_a$ restricted to that block.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The chain rule identity is Lemma~\ref{lem:fiber} applied to the pair statistic
$(r_a, \jt_S)$, identified with $\jt_{\{a\} \cup S}$ via
Proposition~\ref{prop:relabel}; the inequality is Theorem~\ref{thm:subadd},
i.e.\ non-negativity of $I(r_a ; \jt_S)$.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:increment} forecloses an attractive heuristic. One might
expect a dial that is nearly constant globally --- and hence of tiny solo
capacity --- to split exactly the right collisions of an existing sub-battery and
so contribute far more jointly than alone. Conditioning can only decrease the
capacity of a statistic, so this is impossible: no dial ever contributes more to
a battery than it is worth by itself.

\section{Auditing the measurement}

\subsection{The trend and the ceilings were forced}

The three sub-batteries are nested, so Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} forces
$C(S_1) \le C(S_2) \le C(S_3)$: the observation
$7.9455 \le 10.4462 \le 12.1080$ carries no information beyond a check that the
pipeline computes a monotone quantity monotonically.

Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} forces each value below its cell-count logarithm, with
integer certificates $2^{8} \le 713$, $2^{12} \le 6417$, $2^{15} \le 51336$,
giving $\log_2 713 \ge 8 > 7.9455$, $\log_2 6417 \ge 12 > 10.4462$ and
$\log_2 51336 \ge 15 > 12.1080$. The actual ceilings are
$\log_2 713 = 9.4781\ldots$, $\log_2 6417 = 12.6479\ldots$ and
$\log_2 51336 = 15.6478\ldots$.

Theorem~\ref{thm:pop} forces each value below $\log_2 N$; read backwards, the
top value implies $N \ge 2^{12.1080} \approx 4415$.

\begin{theorem}[audited scaling]\label{thm:audit}
Let $\Omega$ be any finite nonempty population and let $d_0, d_1, d_2, d_3$ be
any dials on $\Omega$ of moduli $31, 23, 9, 8$. Put $S_1 = \{0,1\}$,
$S_2 = \{0,1,2\}$ and $S_3 = \{0,1,2,3\}$. Then
\[
C(S_1) \le C(S_2) \le C(S_3),
\]
\[
C(S_1) \le \log_2 713, \quad C(S_2) \le \log_2 6417, \quad
C(S_3) \le \log_2 51336, \quad C(S_3) \le \log_2 |\Omega| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity is Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} applied twice; the product bounds are
Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} with $31\cdot 23 = 713$, $31 \cdot 23 \cdot 9 = 6417$ and
$31 \cdot 23 \cdot 9 \cdot 8 = 51336$; the last is Theorem~\ref{thm:pop}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The shortfalls are not forced}

By Corollary~\ref{cor:shortfall} the ceilings are attainable, so the gaps
\[
9.478 - 7.946 = 1.532, \qquad 12.648 - 10.446 = 2.202, \qquad 15.648 - 12.108 = 3.540
\]
bits are measurements. Each is a log-scale count of collisions: if $\jt_S$
attains $K_S$ of $M_S$ cells then $C(S) \le \log_2 K_S$, so a shortfall of
$\delta$ bits implies at most $M_S 2^{-\delta}$ cells are occupied, or that the
occupied cells are unevenly loaded. The gaps \emph{grow} with the battery ---
$1.53$, $2.20$, $3.54$ --- the signature of an increasingly sparse table: with
$N \approx 4\,400$ rows the first sub-battery has more rows than cells
($4400 > 713$) while the last has far more cells than rows
($51\,336 \gg 4\,400$), so the binding constraint migrates from the
multiplicative ceiling to the sample ceiling as dials are added.

\subsection{The per-dial spread separates saturation from blindness}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:eleven}
Any dial of modulus $11$ on any finite nonempty population has capacity
$c \le \log_2 11 < 3.46$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The image has at most $11$ elements, so Theorem~\ref{thm:maxent} gives
$c \le \log_2 11$; and the integer comparison $11^{50} < 2^{173}$ gives
$50 \log_2 11 < 173$, i.e.\ $\log_2 11 < 3.46$.
\end{proof}

Since $\log_2 11 = 3.45943\ldots$, the reported $3.46$ bits is a rounding of a
value within $0.0006$ bits of its ceiling: this dial is \emph{saturated}.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:thirtyone}
Any dial of modulus $31$ has capacity $c \le \log_2 31$, and $\log_2 31 > 4.9$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The first part is Theorem~\ref{thm:maxent} with $|\img| \le 31$; for the second,
$2^{49} < 31^{10}$ gives $49 < 10 \log_2 31$.
\end{proof}

Since $\log_2 31 = 4.9542\ldots$ and the modulus-$31$ dial reported $0.04$ bits,
that dial sits at about $0.8\%$ of its ceiling: \emph{nearly blind}, not
resolution-limited.

\subsection{The budget as a falsification test, and a detected inconsistency}

Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} is a hard constraint linking the per-dial table to the
joint table, and it can fail. With the reported $c_{31} = 0.04$ bits and the most
generous conceivable $c_{23} \le \log_2 23 = 4.5236$,
\[
C(\{31,23\}) \;\le\; c_{31} + c_{23} \;\le\; 0.04 + 4.5236 \;=\; 4.5636 \text{ bits,}
\]
whereas the reported joint value is $7.9455$ bits --- an excess of $3.38$ bits.
Equivalently, by Corollary~\ref{cor:increment} the modulus-$31$ dial cannot
contribute more than $0.04$ bits to any battery. We conclude, with no statistical
assumption whatsoever, that the per-dial figures and the joint figures cannot
both describe the same dials on the same population: either the per-dial table
was collected under a different configuration (the joint measurement is described
as being taken on a fresh, independent population, and the per-dial list contains
a modulus-$11$ dial absent from the four-dial joint battery), or the $0.04$
figure is attached to the wrong dial. A monotone trend can be ``confirmed'' by a
pipeline silently comparing quantities measured under different conditions; an
inequality between those quantities cannot be.

\section{The ``wall'' is an imbalance meter}

\begin{theorem}[binary law]\label{thm:binary}
Let $f : \Omega \to A$ attain exactly two values $a \ne b$, and let
$p = n_a/N$. Then
\[
C(f) = h(p) := -p\log_2 p - (1-p)\log_2(1-p).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The defining sum has two terms with probabilities $p$ and $1-p$, since
$n_a + n_b = N$, and $\log(N/n_a) = \log(1/p)$, $\log(N/n_b) = \log(1/(1-p))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[one-bit cap]
A statistic with at most two attained values has capacity at most $1$ bit.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[strict monotonicity in the imbalance]
$h$ is strictly increasing on $[0, 1/2]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[wall inversion]\label{thm:inversion}
If $p, q \in [0,1/2]$ and $h(p) = h(q)$ then $p = q$: a reported binary capacity
determines the class imbalance uniquely, and is thus a sufficient statistic for
the split.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[cross-population inversion]
Let $f, g$ be binary statistics on possibly different finite populations with
minority fractions $p, q \in [0,1/2]$. If $C(f) = C(g)$ then $p = q$.
\end{corollary}

Solving $h(p) = 0.4677$ on $[0,1/2]$ gives $p = 0.09959\ldots \approx 9.96\%$:
the ``wall'' is a thermometer, and it reads that the which-factor split divides
the population approximately $10\%/90\%$. This is a falsifiable prediction with
no free parameters. Note that the sample ceiling $\log_2 N \approx 12.1$ bits
does not bind on a binary statistic at all; what binds is the one-bit cap, and
the residual gap from $1$ to $0.4677$ is pure imbalance.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The theory above establishes, for arbitrary finite populations and arbitrary
batteries: monotone scaling, a strict scaling criterion, the multiplicative
ceiling with an attainment witness, the sample ceiling, a per-dial budget, and
the exact identification of a binary wall with the binary entropy of the class
imbalance. This is precisely the \emph{structural} content of a
``scaling is confirmed'' verdict. What measurement alone cannot decide is how
much of the multiplicative ceiling a real population can reach.

\begin{problem}[sample-limited capacity law]
For a uniformly random reading table with $N$ rows and $M$ cells the expected
number of occupied cells is $M(1 - (1-1/M)^N)$, and the capacity is squeezed
between $\log_2$ of that count and $\log_2 N$. Both ceilings are theorems; the
missing piece is a concentration-based lower bound, targeting an asymptotic law
$C = \log_2 N - \Delta(N/M) + o(1)$ with $\Delta$ explicit and vanishing as
$N/M \to 0$.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[exact saturation threshold]
Saturation is equivalent to injectivity of the joint reading, which by the
Chinese Remainder Theorem holds as soon as the population injects into
$\prod \mathbb{Z}/m_i$ with pairwise coprime $m_i$. For non-coprime moduli the
true ceiling is $\log_2 \lcm(m_i)$; making Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} exact in that
generality is a clean self-contained refinement.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[reverse budget]
Subadditivity is very lossy as an upper bound for a battery with an eighty-fold
per-dial spread, and says nothing from below. Bound the joint capacity
\emph{below} by the largest per-dial value plus the conditional information of
the remaining dials, making Corollary~\ref{cor:increment} quantitative;
Lemma~\ref{lem:fiber} already isolates the conditional term required. A companion
question is how large a per-dial spread is compatible with a given joint value:
the joint value is capped by $\sum_i \min\{c_i, \log_2 m_i\}$, which is the
inequality that detected the inconsistency above.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[wall inversion as a statistical test]
Convert Theorem~\ref{thm:inversion} into a hypothesis test by error propagation:
$h'(p) = \log_2\frac{1-p}{p}$ converts a capacity uncertainty $\pm\varepsilon$
into an imbalance uncertainty $\pm\varepsilon/h'(p)$, which near $p = 0.1$ is
$\pm\varepsilon/3.17$.
\end{problem}

\section{Conclusion}

Joint capacity of a battery of bounded-modulus dials is monotone under
enlargement, strictly so under an explicit separation criterion; it is bounded
by the logarithm of the number of cells and, independently, by the logarithm of
the population size; it is bounded by the sum of per-dial capacities; and the
cell-count bound is attained exactly on a Chinese Remainder population, so
shortfalls against it are measurements rather than artefacts. For binary
readouts the theory reduces to the binary entropy function, invertible on the
balanced side, making a reported ``wall'' an exact report of a class imbalance.
Applied to a four-dial battery of moduli $31, 23, 9, 8$, the analysis shows that
the trend and ceiling compliance were guaranteed in advance, while the
substantive content lies in the growing shortfalls, in the certified contrast
between a saturated modulus-$11$ dial and a nearly blind modulus-$31$ dial, and
in the inversion of a $0.4677$-bit wall into a $10\%/90\%$ population split. The
same theory detects what no trend analysis could: the reported per-dial and joint
figures violate the per-dial budget by $3.38$ bits and therefore cannot refer to
the same dials on the same population.

